const r1={id:"R001",inputs:["H003","H004","H005","H006"],output:"N001",definitions:["D001","D002","D004","D005","D006"],rules:["direct","natural_induction","forall_introduction","implication_introduction"],locals:[],applications:[],argument:[
S("S001","The representation carries brackets to operator commutators. H005 and H006 therefore give the following inductions. The h identity starts at hv=lambda v, and h y^(t+1)v=y h y^t v-2y^(t+1)v=(lambda-2(t+1))y^(t+1)v. For x the base t=1 is xyv=yxv+hv=lambda v. The step for t>=1 is xy^(t+1)v=yxy^t v+hy^t v=[t(lambda-t+1)+(lambda-2t)]y^t v=(t+1)(lambda-t)y^t v. The temporary induction hypotheses for these two steps are discharged by natural induction. Thus F001 holds for all natural t, with the x formula used only for t>=1.",["H005","H006"],["act","x","y","h","v","lambda","t"],[],[F("F001","For all natural t, hy^t v=(lambda-2t)y^t v; for every t>=1, xy^t v=t(lambda-t+1)y^(t-1)v.",["h","y","x","v","lambda"],[B("t","natural number")])],["L1","L2"],["L1","L2"]),
S("S002","H003 gives lambda>=0, so |I|=lambda+1=n>0. Iterating F001, for i in I, x^i y^i v=i! lambda(lambda-1)...(lambda-i+1)v!=0: when i=0 the product is 1; otherwise all i factors of the descending product are positive integers and v!=0 by H006. Thus e_i!=0. To prove independence fix any coefficient family c indexed by I and temporarily assume L3: sum_(i in I)c_i e_i=0. For any j in I, apply product_(k in I,k!=j)(h-(lambda-2k)Id_V). The h formula of F001 kills every summand except j; its multiplier on e_j is product_(k!=j)2(k-j), nonzero over C. Hence c_j=0. Since j was arbitrary all coefficients vanish; discharge L3. H004 gives dim_C V=n, so the n independent vectors e_i form a basis.",["H003","H004","H006"],["I","n","lambda","e","x","y","h","v","V","i","j","c"],["F001"],[F("F002","n>0, |I|=n, and (e_i)_(i in I) is a complex basis of V.",["n","I","e","V"],[B("i","element of I")])],["L3"],["L3"]),
S("S003","Set z=y^(lambda+1)v. By F001, hz=(-lambda-2)z. Expand z in the basis F002; none of the eigenvalues lambda-2i for i in I equals -lambda-2. Applying h-(-lambda-2)Id to this expansion and using independence makes every coefficient zero, so z=0. Every higher lowering power vanishes. Dividing the identities in F001 by i! now gives xe_0=0, xe_i=(lambda-i+1)e_(i-1) for i>=1 in I, ye_i=(i+1)e_(i+1) for i<lambda, ye_lambda=0 and he_i=(lambda-2i)e_i.",[],["z","y","v","lambda","h","V","e","x","I","i"],["F001","F002"],[F("F003","y^t v=0 for all t>=lambda+1. On (e_i)_(i in I), xe_0=0, xe_i=(lambda-i+1)e_(i-1) for i>=1, ye_i=(i+1)e_(i+1) for i<lambda, ye_lambda=0, and he_i=(lambda-2i)e_i.",["y","v","lambda","e","I","x","h"],[B("t","natural number"),B("i","element of I")])]),
S("S004","Iterate F003 r times and divide by r!. If r<=i, the x coefficient is (lambda-i+1)...(lambda-i+r)/r!=binom(lambda-i+r,r). If i+r<=lambda, the y coefficient is (i+1)...(i+r)/r!=binom(i+r,r). If r>i the x string reaches xe_0=0, and if i+r>lambda the y string reaches ye_lambda=0. These are integer binomial coefficients, including r=0 where each empty product is 1. Together with F001-F003 these give every clause of N001.",[],["r","i","lambda","x","y","X","Y","e","I","n","V","v","h"],["F001","F002","F003"])
]};
