const r2={id:"R002",inputs:["H001","H003","H005","H007","N001"],output:"N002",definitions:["D001","D002","D003","D004","D005","D006","D007","D008","D009","D010"],rules:["direct","exists_elimination","forall_introduction","implication_introduction"],locals:[],applications:[],argument:[
S("S001","By H007, every X_r,Y_r preserves M. In particular x=X_1 and y=Y_1 preserve M; H005 gives h=xy-yx as operators on V, so h also preserves M. Their restrictions satisfy the same commutator identities. H001 makes R the subring obtained by inverting integers prime to p. H007 supplies an integer basis of M that is a complex basis of V; N001 gives complex dimension n and |I|=n, so that basis has n elements and can be indexed by I.",["H001","H005","H007","N001"],["X","Y","x","y","h","M","V","R","p","n","I"],[],[F("F001","There exists an integer basis a=(a_i)_(i in I) of M which is also a complex basis of V. All X_r,Y_r, x,y,h preserve M and the restricted generators satisfy the Chevalley relations.",["M","I","V","X","Y","x","y","h"],[B("a","family of vectors of M indexed by I"),B("i","element of I"),B("r","natural number")])]),
S("S002","Choose such a basis a from F001. Its tensor images 1 tensor a_i form an R-basis of M_R: the integer coordinate maps of a tensor to mutually inverse maps between M_R and R^I, sending sum_(i in I) d_i tensor a_i to (d_i)_(i in I). Under iota this basis maps to a_i. If a finite R-linear combination maps to zero, its coefficients vanish by complex independence of a. Hence iota is injective, its image is the R-span of a, and M_R is free of rank n. The image complex-linearly spans V because a is a complex basis. Each X_r,Y_r is complex-linear and preserves M by F001, so preserves this R-span.",[],["a","I","M","M_R","R","iota","V","n","X","Y"],["F001"],[F("F002","iota is injective; M_R is free over R of rank n, iota(M_R) complex-linearly spans V, and all X_r,Y_r preserve iota(M_R).",["iota","M_R","R","n","V","X","Y"],[B("r","natural number")])]),
S("S003","Fix i,j in I. The basis formulas in N001 give Q=Y_lambda X_lambda=E_(lambda,lambda): X_lambda kills e_k for k<lambda and sends e_lambda to e_0, which Y_lambda sends back to e_lambda. They also give X_(lambda-i)Q=E_(i,lambda), since the surviving coefficient is binom(lambda-i,lambda-i)=1. Finally Q Y_(lambda-j)=binom(lambda,j)E_(lambda,j). Indeed an input e_k with k<j goes below e_lambda and is killed by Q; for k>j it goes past the lowering string and vanishes; and e_j maps with coefficient binom(lambda,lambda-j)=binom(lambda,j). These statements include lambda=0. By H001 and H003, lambda!, j!, (lambda-j)! have no prime factor p. The factorial identity lambda!=binom(lambda,j)j!(lambda-j)! then shows p does not divide binom(lambda,j), so its rational inverse lies in R.",["N001","H001","H003"],["i","j","I","lambda","X","Y","E","Q","e","p","R"],[],[F("F003","For all i,j in I, binom(lambda,j) is a unit of R and Y_lambda X_lambda=E_(lambda,lambda), X_(lambda-i)E_(lambda,lambda)=E_(i,lambda), E_(lambda,lambda)Y_(lambda-j)=binom(lambda,j)E_(lambda,j).",["I","lambda","R","X","Y","E"],[B("i","element of I"),B("j","element of I")])]),
S("S004","Composing the three identities in F003 and using the defining action of matrix units gives E_ij=binom(lambda,j)^(-1)X_(lambda-i)Y_lambda X_lambda Y_(lambda-j) for every i,j in I. All factors preserve iota(M_R) by F002, and the scalar is in R by F003, hence every E_ij preserves it. F001-F003 and this formula establish N002. No weights modulo p were separated.",[],["i","j","I","lambda","E","X","Y","R","iota","M_R"],["F001","F002","F003"])
]};
