const r3={id:"R003",inputs:["H001","N001","N002","M001","M002"],output:"N003",definitions:["D004","D005","D006","D007","D008","D009","D010","D011","D012"],rules:["direct","forall_introduction","exists_elimination"],locals:[],applications:[],argument:[
S("S001","By N001, the e_i form a basis. By N002, E_ii projects iota(M_R) onto J_i e_i and E_ij carries c e_j to c e_i while preserving iota(M_R); therefore J_j is contained in J_i for every i,j in I. Reversing indices gives equality. The set I contains 0 since n=lambda+1>0, so the common module is J. Each J_i is an R-submodule, and multiplication by e_i identifies it with the image of the R-linear projection E_ii on the finite free module iota(M_R). Images of a finite generating set generate the image, so J is finitely generated. It is nonzero: if every coordinate projection were zero, all of iota(M_R) would be zero, contradicting its complex span V and dim V=n>0. Finally a vector in iota(M_R) is the sum of its E_ii projections, all in J e_i, and conversely every such finite sum belongs to iota(M_R); independence of the e_i makes this sum direct.",["N001","N002"],["I","e","E","iota","M_R","J_i","J","R","V","n","lambda"],[],[F("F001","All J_i equal the nonzero finitely generated R-submodule J of C, and iota identifies M_R with the direct sum of J e_i over i in I.",["J_i","J","R","iota","M_R","e","I"],[B("i","element of I")])]),
S("S002","Define T=Q tensor_R J. Its multiplication map mu:T->C sends q tensor c to qc. It is injective by the fraction description of localization: any element is a finite sum of pure tensors, and a common nonzero integer denominator d rewrites it as (1/d) tensor c' for some c' in J, because d times each rational coefficient belongs to Z, hence R. If its product in C vanishes, c'=0, so the tensor is zero. This also exhibits Q as the fraction field of R and realizes T as the localization of the torsion-free module J inside C. The images 1 tensor c of a finite generating set of J span T over Q, so T is finite dimensional. Tensoring the finite direct decomposition in F001 gives Q tensor_R M_R isomorphic to product_(i in I)T: the forward map consists of tensoring each coordinate c e_i -> c, and the inverse adds the coordinate inclusions c -> c e_i. On pure tensors their two composites are the identity, hence on every tensor by linearity.",[],["T","mu","J","R","iota","M_R","I","e"],["F001"],[F("F002","T=Q tensor_R J is finite dimensional over Q; mu:T->C, q tensor c |-> qc, is injective; and Q tensor_R M_R is Q-linearly isomorphic to the finite product T^I.",["T","mu","J","R","M_R","I"])]),
S("S003","Set r=dim_Q T, a finite natural number by F002. Apply M002 with source B=R, A=Q and source module M_R. N002 provides its free R-basis of n elements, so finrank_R M_R=n and M002 gives dim_Q(Q tensor_R M_R)=n. The rings R and Q are commutative domains and satisfy the strong rank condition: for R, clear denominators to preserve injectivity after passage to its fraction field Q, then apply finite-dimensional linear algebra over Q; for Q use that same dimension inequality directly. Apply M001 over Q to the constant family T indexed by I. F002 supplies finite dimensionality and hence a finite basis of T, while N001 supplies |I|=n. Thus dim_Q(T^I)=sum_(i in I)r=nr. The isomorphism in F002 now gives n=nr; n>0 by N001, whence r=1. Together with injectivity of mu, J therefore spans a one-dimensional rational line in C.",["N002","N001","M002","M001"],["r","T","mu","R","M_R","n","I","J"],["F002"],[F("F003","dim_Q(Q tensor_R J)=1, mu is injective, and the Q-linear span of J in C is one-dimensional.",["J","R","mu"])]),
S("S004","F001 and F003 imply existence of a nonzero a in J and a nonempty finite list of nonzero rational scalars q_1,...,q_k for which q_1 a,...,q_k a generate J over R: start with any nonzero a in J, use the one-dimensional rational span to express a finite generating list as rational multiples of a, and discard zero entries. Each nonzero rational q_j admits q_j=p^(eta_j)u_j with eta_j an integer and u_j a unit of R. Indeed remove the maximal powers of the prime p from the nonzero numerator and denominator; their difference is eta_j and the remaining numerator and denominator are prime to p, making both u_j and its inverse elements of R. Thus the tuple of data specified in F004 exists.",["H001"],["J","R","p"],["F001","F003"],[F("F004","There exist a nonzero a in J, a positive integer k, nonzero rational scalars q_j, integers eta_j and units u_j of R for 1<=j<=k such that q_j=p^(eta_j)u_j and the vectors q_j a generate J over R.",["J","R","p"],[B("a","complex number"),B("k","positive integer"),B("q_j","family of nonzero rationals indexed by {1,...,k}"),B("eta_j","family of integers indexed by {1,...,k}"),B("u_j","family of units of R indexed by {1,...,k}"),B("j","integer with 1<=j<=k")])]),
S("S005","Choose a,k,q,eta,u with the properties in F004. Define nu=min_(1<=j<=k) eta_j and b=p^nu a. Every generator q_j a=p^(eta_j-nu)u_j b belongs to Rb because eta_j-nu>=0. An index attaining the minimum has q_j a=u_j b, and u_j is an R-unit, so b lies in J and this one generator generates Rb. Thus J=Rb, and b!=0 since p is a nonzero integer and a!=0. F001 now gives iota(M_R)=direct_sum_(i in I)Rb e_i. The vectors b e_i are independent by N001 and b!=0 and span by that equality, so form an R-basis. N002 makes iota an isomorphism onto its image, which supplies the equivalent R-basis of M_R. The witnesses are existentially closed to give N003, together with F001-F003.",["N001","N002"],["a","k","q","eta","u","nu","b","p","J","R","I","e","iota","M_R"],["F001","F002","F003","F004"])
]};
