S("S001","By H001-H002, an integer denominator not divisible by p has nonzero image in K and therefore an inverse. Thus the integer map extends uniquely to bar:R->K, bar(a/d)=a_K/d_K. Define Phi:V_K->M_R tensor_R K by m tensor c |-> (1 tensor m) tensor c; define its inverse on pure tensors by (s tensor m) tensor c |-> m tensor (bar(s)c). The tensor balancing relations make these well-defined K-linear maps, and their composites are the identity on pure tensors, hence on all elements. Since p maps to zero, define Psi:M_R tensor_R K -> (M_R/pM_R) tensor_(F_p) K by u tensor c |-> [u] tensor c; its inverse sends [u] tensor c to u tensor c. Changing u by p times a vector changes this by zero, and the F_p balancing relation follows from the R balancing relation and bar modulo p. Conversely R/pR=F_p because every denominator is prime to p, so every R scalar has the same reduction as an element of F_p. These maps are mutually inverse on pure tensors. N002 supplies preservation of M by all lattice operators, and H005 their Chevalley relations, so the actions rho and the operators X_K,Y_K are well-defined and all these tensor maps commute with them.",["H001","H002","H005","N002"],["R","K","p","bar","Phi","Psi","V_K","M_R","M","F_p","rho","X_K","Y_K","L_K"],[],[F("F001","bar:R->K is the extension of the integer map. Phi:V_K -> M_R tensor_R K and Psi:M_R tensor_R K -> (M_R/pM_R) tensor_(F_p) K are K-linear isomorphisms with the pure-tensor formulas and inverses just given; they intertwine all induced lattice operators.",["bar","R","K","Phi","Psi","V_K","M_R","p","F_p","rho","X_K","Y_K"])]),
S("S002","Choose b from N003, so b!=0 and the vectors b e_i form an R-basis of iota(M_R). Define a_i=iota^(-1)(b e_i) in M_R and beta_i=Phi^(-1)(a_i tensor 1) in V_K. The a_i form an R-basis by N002-N003. Its coordinate maps and the map (c_i) -> sum_i a_i tensor c_i are inverse K-linear maps between K^I and M_R tensor_R K; on each pure tensor this is precisely the coordinate expansion. Hence the beta_i form a K-basis. Likewise reducing the coordinates modulo p shows that the classes of a_i form an F_p-basis of M_R/pM_R, whose scalar extension gives this same basis via Psi. N001 gives |I|=n=lambda+1>0. Thus dim_K V_K=n>0.",["N003","N002","N001"],["b","a","beta","iota","e","I","M_R","Phi","Psi","K","V_K","F_p","p","n","lambda"],["F001"],[F("F002","beta=(beta_i)_(i in I) is a K-basis of V_K obtained from the R-basis a_i=iota^(-1)(b e_i), with b!=0. In particular dim_K V_K=n=lambda+1>0.",["beta","I","K","V_K","a","iota","b","e","n","lambda"],[B("i","element of I")])]),
S("S003","The matrices in N001 on e_i are unchanged on b e_i, because all the operators are complex-linear. They therefore are the matrices on a_i over R and on beta_i after applying bar. In particular the formulas for x_K,y_K,h_K in N004 follow with coefficients reduced into K. N002 shows that E_ij preserves iota(M_R). Define T_ij by its induced action after pulling back through iota and then extending scalars using Phi. It sends beta_k to beta_i if k=j and zero otherwise. The full identity in N002 transports to T_ij=bar(binom(lambda,j))^(-1) X_(lambda-i)^K Y_lambda^K X_lambda^K Y_(lambda-j)^K. The scalar bar(binom(lambda,j)) is nonzero, since binom(lambda,j) is a unit of R by N002 and a unital homomorphism to a field sends a unit to a unit.",["N001","N002"],["T","beta","b","a","e","bar","iota","Phi","E","I","R","K","lambda","rho","x_K","y_K","h_K","X_K","Y_K"],["F001","F002"],[F("F003","For all i,j in I, T_ij beta_k equals beta_i when k=j and zero otherwise. Also T_ij=bar(binom(lambda,j))^(-1)X_(lambda-i)^K Y_lambda^K X_lambda^K Y_(lambda-j)^K, where the displayed binomial coefficient has nonzero image in K. The generator matrices on beta are exactly the reduced generator matrices on e in N001.",["T","beta","I","lambda","bar","K","X_K","Y_K","x_K","y_K","h_K","e"],[B("i","element of I"),B("j","element of I"),B("k","element of I")])]),
S("S004","For any integer r with 0<=r<=lambda, H003 and H001 imply p does not divide r!, so H002 gives r! !=0 in K. The equality r! X_r=x^r on V restricts to the same equality on M by N002 and reduces to r! X_r^K=x_K^r on V_K. Division yields X_r^K=(r!)^(-1)x_K^r, and the identical argument gives Y_r^K=(r!)^(-1)y_K^r. F001-F003 and these formulas prove every clause of N004, existentially closing b,a,beta,T. When lambda=0, all factorials and binomial coefficients here are 1. No assumption on algebraic closure of K has been used.",["H001","H002","H003","N002"],["r","lambda","p","K","X","Y","x","y","M","V","V_K","X_K","Y_K","x_K","y_K","beta","T","b","a"],["F001","F002","F003"])
